WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:05.520
Mind Your Decisions, I'm Presh Talwalkar. Start with rectangle ABCD.

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:11.399
Construct an overlapping semicircle CPS as shown, if

00:00:12.549 --> 00:00:20.009
CQ has a length equal to 10. The question is what is the area of rectangle ABCD? I

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:23.850
adapted this problem from Aman's Maths Blogs.

00:00:24.609 --> 00:00:31.169
Pause the video if you'd like to give this problem a try, and when you're ready, keep watching to learn how to solve this problem.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.160
So, how can we solve this problem?

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:51.680
We will get started by considering O, the center of the semicircle. If

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:59.449
the semicircle has a radius equal to r, then OS is equal to r, and CD is also equal to r.

00:00:59.699 --> 00:01:01.699
That's one side of the rectangle.

00:01:02.519 --> 00:01:06.919
We also have OQ is equal to r and OC is equal to r.

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:13.309
Now suppose that the rectangle has another side length AD that's equal to x.

00:01:13.979 --> 00:01:20.329
That means BC also has a length equal to x and OB has a length of x minus r.

00:01:21.869 --> 00:01:27.769
We'll now consider that the area of the rectangle will be one side multiplied by its other side,

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.469
so it will be equal to x times r

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:37.609
We want to figure out x times r, and this is how we'll do it. We'll consider the right triangle OBQ.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:46.129
We can solve for BQ using the Gogu theorem. BQ squared is equal to r squared minus the quantity x minus r squared.

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:52.490
We'll simplify this a little bit and we get BQ squared is equal to 2xr minus x squared.

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.040
We'll put this equation to the side.

00:01:55.530 --> 00:02:00.470
We then go back to the rectangle and we consider the right triangle CBQ.

00:02:01.229 --> 00:02:07.819
We can again solve for BQ, by the Gogu theorem. BQ squared is equal to 10 squared minus x squared.

00:02:08.489 --> 00:02:14.719
We thus have two equations for BQ squared. We can set them equal to each other. Now recall,

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:17.929
we want to solve for the area which is x multiplied by r.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:25.070
In this equation, the negative x squared terms will cancel out, so we get 2xr is equal to 10 squared.

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:32.809
We then divide both sides by 2, and we solve that xr is equal to 50, and that's the area of the rectangle.

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:35.190
Incredible!

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:42.859
Thanks for making Mind Your Decisions one of the best channels on YouTube. As always, thanks for watching and thanks for your support.

